        side is handled by an <strong>Animator Controller</strong> — a visual state machine with
        clips for idle, for walking in each direction, and for the attack. Your C# script
        communicates with it by setting <strong>parameters</strong>, and the controller decides
        which clip that means. These are the methods that do the talking:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Unity API</th><th>Signature</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>animator.SetBool()</code></td>
            <td><code>void SetBool(string name, bool value)</code></td>
            <td>Sets a boolean parameter. For <em>persistent, continuous</em> states — idle versus walking. True and false are both meaningful values.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>animator.SetInteger()</code></td>
            <td><code>void SetInteger(string name, int value)</code></td>
            <td>Sets a whole-number parameter. For <em>discrete multi-state</em> selection such as a direction (0, 1, 2). Each value is a different clip.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>animator.SetFloat()</code></td>
            <td><code>void SetFloat(string name, float value)</code></td>
