    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Inspect the Controller You Are Driving</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Before you write a line, go and read the machine you are about to talk to. Someone built
        this state machine for you; your job is only to drive it, and you cannot drive something
        you have not looked at. In the Overworld scene, select the child <strong>Sprite</strong>
        object under Player — from <a href="1.2.html">1.2</a>, that is where the Animator lives,
        not on the Player root — then open <strong>Window → Animation → Animator</strong>.
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>Click the <strong>Parameters</strong> tab in the top-left of the Animator window. Three parameters are already configured: <code>IsWalking</code> (Bool), <code>WalkDir</code> (Int), and <code>Attack</code> (Trigger). What are the exact spellings, including capitals? Write them down — you will type them into strings, and Unity will not forgive a typo.</li>
        <li>Click the arrow from <code>Walk_Horiz</code> to <code>Idle_Horiz</code> and read its condition in the Inspector. Which parameter does it test, and what value does it want?</li>
        <li>Click the arrows between the up, side, and down states. They compare <code>WalkDir</code> to <code>0</code>, <code>1</code>, and <code>2</code>. Which number means which direction? You need these exact values in your code, and the wrong one is a character that walks backwards.</li>
        <li>Now click the arrow into any of the <code>Shovel_Swing</code> states. What two conditions must <em>both</em> be true before the attack animation plays?</li>
        <li>Look at the Animator component in the Inspector. Which controller asset is assigned to it, and what kind of parameter — Bool, Int, Float, or Trigger — is <em>missing</em> from this controller entirely?</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Test It, Then Break It</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save your script, return to Unity, press Play, and walk in all four directions. Your
        character should blend between idle, walking sideways, walking up, and walking down, with
        the sprite flipping as it changes direction. Click the mouse to swing. Once that works,
        the section below is where you prove you know <em>why</em> — by breaking one piece at a
        time and predicting what will happen before you press Play.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Diagnosing Animation Faults</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Misspell <code>"IsWalking"</code> as <code>"isWalking"</code> (lowercase i). What does the Unity Console report? Why is a misspelled parameter a warning rather than a crash — and why is that a mixed blessing for a beginner?</li>
            <li><strong>Delete the <code>UpdateAnimation();</code> call</strong> from <code>Update()</code> and press Play. No errors appear and the character still moves. What does it look like, and what does that prove about methods you write yourself?</li>
            <li>Comment out both <code>spriteRenderer.flipX</code> lines — <code>true</code> and <code>false</code>. Walk left. Which way is the sprite facing now, and can any input make it face the other way while walking sideways?</li>
            <li>Change <code>GetComponentInChildren&lt;Animator&gt;()</code> to <code>GetComponent&lt;Animator&gt;()</code>. What error appears — and which line does Unity point at, the one in <code>Start()</code> or the one in <code>UpdateAnimation()</code>? What does the answer tell you about <em>when</em> the null was created and when it was first used?</li>
            <li>Set <code>WalkDir</code> to <code>7</code> — a value no transition in the controller checks. What happens to the animation, and what does that teach you about a state machine with no matching transition?</li>
            <li>Click the mouse rapidly, then hold it down instead. Describe the difference between the two, and connect it to <code>GetMouseButtonDown</code> versus <code>GetMouseButton</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Exploring state transitions</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Keep the Animator window open while the game runs in Play mode. Walk around. Watch the blue progress bar slide across each state and the transition arrows light up in real time. Which state is active when you stand still, and which parameter is it testing?</li>
            <li>Hold W and D together and watch <code>WalkDir</code> in the Parameters tab. Which branch of your <code>if</code> chain won, and what would you change in the condition if you wanted vertical movement to win instead?</li>
            <li>Your Player is wired to <code>Player_Shovel.controller</code>, which is why the attack is a shovel swing. Go and find <code>Assets/Artwork/Animations/Top_Down/PlayerAnimator/Axe/Player_Axe.controller</code> and open it. It is a complete, working controller with axe swings instead of shovel swings, and it is assigned to nothing. Which single property on the Animator component would you change to make your character swing an axe? (You will do exactly this in Unit 5.)</li>
            <li>Add a fourth parameter of your own to the controller and wire a transition to it. What is the minimum you need — a parameter, a state, and what else?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Open the Animator window and inspect states, arrows, and parameter conditions</li>
            <li>Explain the distinct purposes of <code>SetBool</code>, <code>SetInteger</code>, <code>SetFloat</code>, and <code>SetTrigger</code> — and why the setter must match the parameter's type</li>
            <li>Explain the architecture split: your code sets state parameters, the Animator Controller plays the clips</li>
            <li>Say which <code>WalkDir</code> integer means up, sideways, and down</li>
            <li>Use <code>Mathf.Abs()</code> to resolve which directional input dominates</li>
            <li><strong>Explain why <code>GetComponent&lt;Animator&gt;()</code> returns null on the Player, and why <code>GetComponentInChildren</code> does not</strong></li>
            <li>Cache component references in <code>Start()</code> rather than looking them up every frame</li>
            <li>Commit your working animation integration to GitHub</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

